    "https://boothuc.ca/about/our-people/faculty-publications/": {
      "title": "Faculty Publications",
      "description": "Faculty Publications - Booth University College Skip to main content Honour and Hope Harvest Dinner | September 25, 2026 — Reserve your seat today View menus ALUMS Apply Visit Donate Log In Populi Corporate Learning Academy National Partners Global Partners Search Search Search About About Overview Our Story Mission and Vision Governance Accreditations and Affiliations Centre for Salvation Army Studies Land Acknowledgement Publications Our People Contact Us Office of the President Staff and Faculty Directory Careers Faculty Publications Our Campus Booth Bistro Food Services Venue Rentals Getting Here Admissions Admissions Overview How to Apply Domestic Undergraduate Applicants International Undergraduate Applicants Continuing Studies Applicants MBA Applicants Admission Requirements High School Students International Students Mature and Transfer Students Special Admission Students English Proficiency Tuition and Financial Aid Tuition and Fees Scholarships and Awards Financial Aid Why Booth UC Salvationist Students International Students For Guidance Counsellors Parents and Supporters Tours and Visits Viewbook Contact Admissions Programs Programs Overview Our Schools School of Business School of Social Work School of Humanities and Social Sciences School for Continuing Studies School of Graduate Studies Explore Programs Academic Calendar Course Directory Experiential Learning Learning Outcomes Academic Excellence Registrar's Office Graduation Requirements Academic Partnerships Academic Innovations Department Pilot Projects Students Students Overview Student Services Health Insurance Mental Wellness Housing Resources International Student Supports IT Support Student Employment Student Policies Student Handbook Academic Support Academic Learning Centre Academic Advising Accessibility Services Get Involved New Students Spiritual Life Fitness and Recreation Student Council Student Governance Positions Library Library Overview John Fairbank Memorial Library Hours and Location Contact Membership and Account Rules and Policies Services Login and Password Help On-Site Services Studying Remotely For Faculty For Alums Request a Resource Resources Find Books & Articles Journal Articles Course Reserves Writing Guides E-Book User Guide Research Guides Business English Film Studies Psychology Religion Salvation Army Social Work Sociology News and Events News and Events Overview News Connect Magazine Subscribe Unsubscribe Events Convocation 2026 Orientations Lecture Series Toolkit Talks Earl Robinson Memorial Lecture Series Booth UC Honour and Hope Harvest Dinner - Sept 25, 2026 Homepage › About › Our People › Faculty Publications Faculty Publications Our Story Our People Contact Us Office of the President Staff and Faculty Directory Careers Faculty Publications Our Campus Close Our faculty present their research in many ways that contribute to knowledge and advancement in their disciplines. We're proud of their scholarly work. This list includes published research, conference presentations, advancing knowledge in areas of practice, teaching, and service. The Role of Research This creative energy finds its way into classroom discussions and student generated research. As a by-product of this emphasis on research, our students participate in the culture of research, are taught by recognized and respected experts in their fields of study and, for those who aspire to graduate studies, are better prepared to move forward into graduate programs. Faculty Research and Publications Report Each year, Booth University College publishes a Faculty Research and Publications Report to showcase how our faculty members are actively engaged in the creation of knowledge, and to profile their publications and professional achievements. Faculty Research and Publications Report Published Work by Booth UC Faculty Access the latest books published by (or with contributions from) Booth University College faculty. Settled Views: The Shorter Writings of Catherine Booth , edited by Andrew M. Eason & Roger J Green, October 2017. Dr. Andrew Eason is Director, School of Humanities and Social Sciences and Director of the Centre for Salvation Army Studies. In September 2016, Dr. Roy Jeal’s essay “Sociorhetorical Intertexture” was published in Exploring Intertextuality: Diverse Strategies for New Testament Use of Texts . In August 2016 Dr. Roy Jeal’s essay “Clothes Make the (Wo)Man” was included in Foundations for Sociorhetorical Exploration: A Rhetoric of Religious Antiquity Reader (RRA4). Saved, Sanctified and Serving: Perspectives on Salvation Army Theology and Practice , by Denis Metrustery – with a chapter by Dr. Andrew Eason, Director, School of Humanities and Social Sciences: “Go into all the world…” An overview of Salvationist Missiology The Book of Revelation: A non-scary approach, by Major Philip Davisson Exploring Philemon: Freedom, Brotherhood, and Partnership in the New Society , by Dr. Roy Jeal, Professor of Religion For His Eyes Only: The Women of James Bond , contributor: Dr. Michael Boyce, Vice President Academic and Dean and Associate Professor, English and Film Studies Program Colossians-Philemon: A Beginning-Intermediate Greek Reader , by Dr. Roy Jeal, Professor of Religion Boundless Salvation: The Shorter Writings of William Booth , co-edited by Dr. Andrew Eason, Director, School of Humanities and Social Sciences and Roger Green, Professor at Gordon College in Massachusetts The Lasting Influence of the War on Postwar British Film , by Dr. Michael Boyce, Vice President Academic and Dean and Associate Professor, English and Film Studies Program When Justice is the Measure , by Dr. James E. Read, Associate Professor of Philosophy and Ethics Ok Cancel 290 Vaughan Street Winnipeg, Manitoba R3B 2N8 CANADA Contact Us Land Acknowledgement Unsubscribe Facebook Instagram Youtube LinkedIn Privacy Policy © 2026 Booth University College Booth University College is authorized to grant degrees, diplomas, and certificates of standing by the Province of Manitoba through The Degree Granting Act and The Salvation Army William and Catherine Booth University College Incorporation Act. We use cookies to ensure you get the best experience on our website. By using this site, you agree to these cookies being set. To learn more, read our privacy policy. View policy Ok"
    },
    "https://boothuc.ca/assets/content/BUC%20Job%20Posting_INSTRUCTORS%20(Faculty%20for%20MBA)_Part-Time-Feb%202026.pdf": {
      "title": "Instructor",
      "description": " \n \n \nBooth University College \n \nInstructor  \n(FACULTY POSITION FOR MBA PROGRAM) \n \nThe MBA Program of Booth University College invites applications for multiple part -time Instructor \npositions in Business Administration (with a variety of specializations in Marketing, Accounting, Data \nAnalyses & Research Methods, CSR, Leadership). The successful candidates will teach, supervise graduate \nstudents and serve on various committees across t he Master of Business Administration program. \nPreference will be given to applicants with demonstrated experience in graduate-level Business programs. \n \nCore Responsibilities:  \n \nTeaching  \nFaculty teaching responsibilities normally include four graduate level courses per year. Additional teaching \nresponsibilities may be assigned as required.  \n \nService  \nThe successful candidates will serve different committees across the Master of Business Administration \nprogram. \n \nAdvising/Supervision \nThe faculty member will be responsible for advising assigned numbers of students in the program provide \nsupervision of final program projects. At half-time employment, faculty will be required to supervise two \nor more graduate level projects per cohort.  \n \nQualifications:  \nAll applicants must possess a PhD/DBA or hold equivalent teaching experience at the graduate level . \nCandidates with work in progress in doctoral studies will be considered if there is evidence of extensive \nteaching experience. (Candidates with a combination of CPA and MBA will be considered for accounting \ncourses with a minimum of five years of executive work experience.) Satisfactory Criminal Record Check \ncertificate must be provided. The successful candidate must have an active Christian faith and a \ncommitment to the integration of faith and learning in a Christian university-level setting. \n \n \n \n\n \n \nTo Apply \n \nApplication materials should include: \n• curriculum vitae,  \n• statement of teaching philosophy,  \n• statement on how your faith commitment would contribute to both teaching and scholarship \nat Booth UC,  \n• names and contact information for three references and  \n• evidence of effective teaching ( provide samples of course syllabi, teaching outlines and other \nmaterials, if available).   \n \nThis is a part -time position at 18.75 hours per week. The anticipated starting date for the position is \nbetween August to September 2026. The position provides the opportunity to work in a stimulating \nuniversity college environment with a mission-driven purpose. \n \nComplete application materials must be submitted in confidence directly to the Human Resources \nGeneralist, at: (email: BoothUC.Careers@BoothUC.ca). \n \nAll qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however in accordance with Canadian Immigration \nrequirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority.  \n \nAccommodations are available upon request throughout the recruitment process. \n \nAbout Booth University College \nBooth University College is a Christian institution centrally located in Winnipeg. The University College is \nrooted in The Salvation Army’s history of meeting the needs of the most vulnerable in society and are \ncommitted to “Education for a Better World” as described in our mission statement: \n \nWilliam and Catherine Booth University College, a Christian university college rooted in The Salvation \nArmy’s Wesleyan theological tradition, brings together Christian faith, rigorous scholarship and a passion \nfor service. The University College educates s tudents to understand the complexities of our world, to \ndevelop the knowledge and skills necessary to be active contributors to society, and to know how Christian \nfaith compels them to bring hope, social justice and mercy into our world. \n \n  "
    },
    "https://boothuc.ca/about/our-people/careers/": {
      "title": "Careers",
      "description": "Careers - Booth University College Skip to main content Honour and Hope Harvest Dinner | September 25, 2026 — Reserve your seat today View menus ALUMS Apply Visit Donate Log In Populi Corporate Learning Academy National Partners Global Partners Search Search Search About About Overview Our Story Mission and Vision Governance Accreditations and Affiliations Centre for Salvation Army Studies Land Acknowledgement Publications Our People Contact Us Office of the President Staff and Faculty Directory Careers Faculty Publications Our Campus Booth Bistro Food Services Venue Rentals Getting Here Admissions Admissions Overview How to Apply Domestic Undergraduate Applicants International Undergraduate Applicants Continuing Studies Applicants MBA Applicants Admission Requirements High School Students International Students Mature and Transfer Students Special Admission Students English Proficiency Tuition and Financial Aid Tuition and Fees Scholarships and Awards Financial Aid Why Booth UC Salvationist Students International Students For Guidance Counsellors Parents and Supporters Tours and Visits Viewbook Contact Admissions Programs Programs Overview Our Schools School of Business School of Social Work School of Humanities and Social Sciences School for Continuing Studies School of Graduate Studies Explore Programs Academic Calendar Course Directory Experiential Learning Learning Outcomes Academic Excellence Registrar's Office Graduation Requirements Academic Partnerships Academic Innovations Department Pilot Projects Students Students Overview Student Services Health Insurance Mental Wellness Housing Resources International Student Supports IT Support Student Employment Student Policies Student Handbook Academic Support Academic Learning Centre Academic Advising Accessibility Services Get Involved New Students Spiritual Life Fitness and Recreation Student Council Student Governance Positions Library Library Overview John Fairbank Memorial Library Hours and Location Contact Membership and Account Rules and Policies Services Login and Password Help On-Site Services Studying Remotely For Faculty For Alums Request a Resource Resources Find Books & Articles Journal Articles Course Reserves Writing Guides E-Book User Guide Research Guides Business English Film Studies Psychology Religion Salvation Army Social Work Sociology News and Events News and Events Overview News Connect Magazine Subscribe Unsubscribe Events Convocation 2026 Orientations Lecture Series Toolkit Talks Earl Robinson Memorial Lecture Series Booth UC Honour and Hope Harvest Dinner - Sept 25, 2026 Homepage › About › Our People › Careers Careers Our Story Our People Contact Us Office of the President Staff and Faculty Directory Careers Faculty Publications Our Campus Close Welcome, and thanks for your interest in joining our community. Booth University College is a growing undergraduate Christian institution built on 40 years of excellence. Our story starts with our commitment: Education for a Better World. Our approach to learning is anchored in academic excellence and based on a vision of hope and social justice. We go beyond preparing students for a career. We prepare them to transform their own communities and the world. At Booth University College, we provide an environment where every voice is heard and valued. Our campus community promotes academic and personal growth. We bring together faith, education and community for an unforgettable and transformative learning experience. Discover the joy of working in a place that prepares students for a life of purpose and service. Our Campus We're located in downtown Winnipeg, Manitoba. Our small campus makes it easy to feel comfortable, safe, and welcomed. People quickly find their way around, make friends, and get to know our students, staff, and growing community. Our neighbouring community plays an important part in the learning journey at Booth UC. In our mission to unite scholarship with a passion for service, our students participate in Urban Service Learning. We enrich the learning experience while serving some the most vulnerable in our community. Employee Benefits We prioritize employee well-being and growth.  Our comprehensive benefits package supports you at every stage of your professional and personal journey. Comprehensive health and dental plan Professional development programs Employee tuition bursary Counselling and mental wellness supports RRSP options Generous vacation time, sick days, and paid/unpaid leaves Long-term disability, life insurance, and accident coverage Discounts in our bistro and nearby fitness centre CURRENT JOB POSTINGS: Urgent Role: Educational Support Technologist Admissions Counsellor Administrative Coordinator - Academic Vice President Academic and Dean Accessibility Manager (Part-Time, Term) Social Work Tutor, Part-Time /Term (2026-2027 Academic Year) Instructor (FACULTY POSITION FOR MBA PROGRAM) Sessional Instructors for MBA President and Vice Chancellor Send inquiries about employment to BoothUC.Careers@boothuc.ca . Booth University College reflects The Salvation Army’s deep involvement in service to the community. Applicants are encouraged to be familiar with our history and our Mission and Vision. Accessible Employment Policy At Booth University College, we aim to remove barriers in our workplace. We're committed to complying with the Accessibility Standard for Employment under The Accessibility for Manitobans Act. Our policies and practices reflect the principles of dignity, independence, integration, and equal opportunity. Read our Accessible Employment Policy Ok Cancel 290 Vaughan Street Winnipeg, Manitoba R3B 2N8 CANADA Contact Us Land Acknowledgement Unsubscribe Facebook Instagram Youtube LinkedIn Privacy Policy © 2026 Booth University College Booth University College is authorized to grant degrees, diplomas, and certificates of standing by the Province of Manitoba through The Degree Granting Act and The Salvation Army William and Catherine Booth University College Incorporation Act. We use cookies to ensure you get the best experience on our website. By using this site, you agree to these cookies being set. To learn more, read our privacy policy. View policy Ok"
    },
    "https://boothuc.ca/students/get-involved/student-governance-positions/": {
      "title": "Student Governance Positions",
      "description": "Student Governance Positions - Booth University College Skip to main content Honour and Hope Harvest Dinner | September 25, 2026 — Reserve your seat today View menus ALUMS Apply Visit Donate Log In Populi Corporate Learning Academy National Partners Global Partners Search Search Search About About Overview Our Story Mission and Vision Governance Accreditations and Affiliations Centre for Salvation Army Studies Land Acknowledgement Publications Our People Contact Us Office of the President Staff and Faculty Directory Careers Faculty Publications Our Campus Booth Bistro Food Services Venue Rentals Getting Here Admissions Admissions Overview How to Apply Domestic Undergraduate Applicants International Undergraduate Applicants Continuing Studies Applicants MBA Applicants Admission Requirements High School Students International Students Mature and Transfer Students Special Admission Students English Proficiency Tuition and Financial Aid Tuition and Fees Scholarships and Awards Financial Aid Why Booth UC Salvationist Students International Students For Guidance Counsellors Parents and Supporters Tours and Visits Viewbook Contact Admissions Programs Programs Overview Our Schools School of Business School of Social Work School of Humanities and Social Sciences School for Continuing Studies School of Graduate Studies Explore Programs Academic Calendar Course Directory Experiential Learning Learning Outcomes Academic Excellence Registrar's Office Graduation Requirements Academic Partnerships Academic Innovations Department Pilot Projects Students Students Overview Student Services Health Insurance Mental Wellness Housing Resources International Student Supports IT Support Student Employment Student Policies Student Handbook Academic Support Academic Learning Centre Academic Advising Accessibility Services Get Involved New Students Spiritual Life Fitness and Recreation Student Council Student Governance Positions Library Library Overview John Fairbank Memorial Library Hours and Location Contact Membership and Account Rules and Policies Services Login and Password Help On-Site Services Studying Remotely For Faculty For Alums Request a Resource Resources Find Books & Articles Journal Articles Course Reserves Writing Guides E-Book User Guide Research Guides Business English Film Studies Psychology Religion Salvation Army Social Work Sociology News and Events News and Events Overview News Connect Magazine Subscribe Unsubscribe Events Convocation 2026 Orientations Lecture Series Toolkit Talks Earl Robinson Memorial Lecture Series Booth UC Honour and Hope Harvest Dinner - Sept 25, 2026 Homepage › Students › Get Involved › Student Governance Positions Student Governance Positions Student Services Academic Support Get Involved New Students Spiritual Life Fitness and Recreation Student Council Student Governance Positions Close Students are valued participants in the University College’s life and direction. Student Member positions have been reserved within two of Booth University College’s primary governing bodies. Please note the descriptions and criteria for both positions below and consider whether you are ready to serve Booth UC in this important function. Student Member Positions on Booth UC Governing Councils Student Member of the Booth UC Senate The Senate is responsible for the academic policies of Booth University College. It makes recommendations to the Board of Trustees about changes to programs, curriculum, courses and degrees. It also determines the academic standards for admission to the University College. The student member of the Senate position is a one-year term. It's voted by members of the Student Council, recommended by the Dean of Students and approved by the Booth UC President, who is also Chair of the Senate. Eligibility criteria for the Student Member of the Senate are: Current enrolment at Booth UC’s Winnipeg campus. Maintain a minimum course load of 9 credit hours per semester. Agreement with and support of the Mission and ethos of Booth UC. Available for Senate meetings from late August to May. Student Member of the Booth UC Board of Trustees The Booth UC Board of Trustees is appointed by the Governing Council of The Salvation Army in Canada. It acts as an independent body, responsible for the governance of the university college. This position is a one-year term. It's voted by members of the Student Council and recommended by the Dean of Students to the Booth UC President. In addition to the criteria for the Student Member of the Senate position, the Student Member of the Board of Trustees must: Be a dedicated Christian. Freely subscribe to the Statement of Faith, believing without reservation the authority of the Scriptures. Agree with the objectives of Booth UC and show an active interest in its operations. Be at least eighteen (18) years of age. Attend all meetings of the Booth UC Board of Trustees, either in person or virtually. How to Apply If you're interested in either of these positions, write a Personal Statement to the Dean of Students . The Personal Statement should include: Name, year of study, degree program Reason(s) why they are applying for the position Contribution(s) they believe they can make in this position Qualifications they bring to this position If you have any questions, contact the Dean of Students . Ok Cancel 290 Vaughan Street Winnipeg, Manitoba R3B 2N8 CANADA Contact Us Land Acknowledgement Unsubscribe Facebook Instagram Youtube LinkedIn Privacy Policy © 2026 Booth University College Booth University College is authorized to grant degrees, diplomas, and certificates of standing by the Province of Manitoba through The Degree Granting Act and The Salvation Army William and Catherine Booth University College Incorporation Act. We use cookies to ensure you get the best experience on our website. By using this site, you agree to these cookies being set. To learn more, read our privacy policy. View policy Ok"
    },
    "https://boothuc.ca/assets/content/BUC%20Job%20Posting_Educational%20Support%20Technologist_FT_May_12_%202026%20(002).pdf": {
      "title": "Booth University College",
      "description": " \nBooth University College \nEducational Support Technologist \n \nAs an integral part of the technology support team serving all areas of campus including academic, and \nadministrative, the Educational Support Technologist provides client-focused support for the installation, \nconfiguration, and ongoing maintenance of end-user technology equipment and applications. The \nEducational Support Technologist acts as an ambassador for the Technology department by building and \nmaintaining relations with Faculty, Staff, Students and other clients, and ensuring consistent and high-\nquality delivery of Information Technology services. \nSUPERVISORY FUNCTIONS: none \nESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS OF THE JOB/ROLES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: \n• Provide technical support to Faculty, Staff, and Students by researching and answering questions; \ntroubleshooting problems; and maintaining systems and technologies \n• Provide training for Faculty/Staff on classroom technology  \n• Support Faculty/Staff/Students through training as needs/technology are required \n• Create, update and maintain technology manuals \n• Check security cameras to confirm they are always working \n• Attend regular Technology Department meetings and participate in other committees and \nmeetings as assigned by your supervisor. \n• Assist with new technology projects and participate in vendor meetings as directed \n• Engage in self-directed training to upgrade present skills and keep current  on technologies \n• Provide after-hours support as required by the supervisor  \n• Communicate with Territorial Headquarters (THQ) Service Desk regarding centrally managed email \nand phone system and other offsite-managed systems \n• The job functions above will support various systems including but not limited to: \no Video and Audio Conferencing \no Student Card access system \no Security cameras \no Recording equipment \no Copiers/printers \no Lab virtual desktops \no Digital signage \no Classroom technology – smart boards, TV screens, Faculty computers, peripheral devices, \nand mobile conferencing technology \no Setup and configuration of new computer equipment as required \n \no Learning Management System Support \no Basic Computer repair (Mac and PC) \no Laptop/desktop imaging \no Maintain computer lab and equipment (Library and ALC \no WIFI – Waldron building and BoothUC Union Station \no Network cabling \no VPN support \no Maintain up to date documentation and diagrams – technology and classroom technology \no Sound systems (portable and chapel)  \no Student Information System \no Software/firmware upgrades \no Server/desktop – updates, repairs, troubleshooting \n \nQUALIFICATIONS \n1. Required \n• Satisfactory criminal record check \n• Fully supportive of the integration of faith and learning in a Christian university-level setting and a \ncommitment to the Mission of Booth University College  \n• Ability to integrate your Christian faith within the workplace \n• Must be valid to work in Canada \n• Two years’ directly related experience dealing with varied technologies \n• Proficient with technology; Able to operate and troubleshoot classroom technology like video \nconferencing systems, screens, computers, and learning management systems \n• Industry certification (A+, Network +, etc.) or related education required \n• A self-starter who is willing to learn and enjoys working independently and in a team environment \n• Knowledge of Windows 11 Pro, Windows Server 2019, 2022 and 2025 \n• The ability to work under pressure and make responsible decisions quickly; able to remain patient \nand persistent when dealing with frustrating problems and problem solve in a creative manner \n• Excellent time management skills - the ability to triage tasks to handle changing priorities and \ndemands \n• Excellent organizational skills in managing classroom resources including books, educational \nmaterials, and equipment \n• A keen interest in keeping up to date with technological developments, in life-long learning, and \nongoing professional development. \n \n• Excellent verbal and written communication skills; an effective communicator, comfortable working \nwith people in a wide variety of jobs and levels and able to express ideas and solutions in clear, \nnon-technical terms to the end-user  \n• Able to demonstrate patience and empathy, while interacting with students from various \nbackgrounds and varying learning abilities. \n• Strong Inter-Personal Skills – able to display positive and professional customer service orientation \nwhile connecting with students, instructors and staff both in-person and remotely \n• A valid driver’s license is required as we provide support for two locations. \n \n2. Assets (Preferred Qualifications) \n• Technical work-related experience in an educational environment is desirable \n• Experience with educational institutions’ varied technologies \n• Microsoft certification or practical work experience \n \nThis is an entry-level, full-time position with 37.5 hours per week.  Booth University College offers a \nstimulating university college environment, a mission-driven purpose, and the opportunity to contribute to \nchange as Booth UC moves forward on its commitment to its mission: Education for a Better World. \n \nApplication Instructions \nApplications may be submitted in confidence to Human Resources; Booth University College by email: \nBoothUC.Careers@boothuc.ca.  Applications must include a cover letter. In the letter describe why you want \nto work at Booth University College. Also, please detail what key strengths you bring to this position .  All \nqualified candidates are encouraged to apply however, in accordance with Canadian Immigration \nrequirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority.   \nAccommodations are available upon request throughout the recruitment process. \n \nAbout Booth University College \nBooth UC is a Christian university college located in Winnipeg, Manitoba (Canada) and rooted in The \nSalvation Army’s history of meeting the needs of the most vulnerable in society. As an open-enrollment \ninstitution, we welcome students from many different backgrounds, cultures, and countries. Deeply \ncommitted to social justice, we blend Christian faith and rigorous scholarship with a passion for service. Our \napproach to learning is anchored in academic excellence and based on a vision of social justice, hope, and \nmercy for all.  We are committed to our mission: Education for a Better World.  This is described in our \nmission statement: \n \nWilliam and Catherine Booth University College, a Christian university college rooted in The Salvation Army’s \nWesleyan theological tradition, brings together Christian faith, rigorous scholarship, and a passion for \nservice. The University College educates students to understand the complexities of our world, to develop \n \nthe knowledge and skills necessary to be active contributors to society, and to know how the Christian faith \ncompels them to bring hope, social justice, and mercy into our world. "
    },
    "https://boothuc.ca/assets/content/BUC%20Job%20Posting_Admissions%20Counsellor_Sept%202026.pdf": {
      "title": "process. This position reports to the Admissions Coordinator and is a key member of the Admissions team.",
      "description": "  \n \nAdmissions Counsellor \n \nThe Admissions Counsellor is responsible for identifying future students as they make key decisions on their \neducational goals, and assisting them from first contact through to enrollment, while meeting targets and \npromoting the advantages of Booth University College.  The individual in this role brings creativity, energy, is \nengaged and is a self-motivated team member with a solid understanding of the post-secondary admissions \nprocess. This position reports to the Admissions Coordinator and is a key member of the Admissions team. \n \nSUPERVISORY FUNCTIONS: none \n \nESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS OF THE JOB/ROLES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: \n• Plan and execute recruitment plans for assigned area and/or target population, developing a strategy \nby creatively utilizing market research, marketing techniques, and knowledge of current best practices \nin post-secondary enrollment; work collaboratively as part of Admissions team to achieve the \ninstitution’s enrollment goals \n• Develop and update a database of and engage with teachers, administrators, and other key \ninfluencers/contact people; engage with these people to increase awareness of Booth UC, identify \npotential applicants, and book workshops, information sessions, and other recruitment events  \n• Develop extensive knowledge of Booth University College academic programs and policies, admissions \nrequirements, campus activities, scholarships, etc. \n• Develop a solid understanding of The Salvation Army and the role of Booth University College within it. \n• Promote and raise the profile of Booth University College through a variety of activities which will \ninclude: \no Public speaking,  \no Attend career symposiums \no Meet one on one with prospective students and parents  \no Organize and lead campus events to increase brand awareness for prospective students \no Coordinate and host individual and group campus visits \no Coordinate off-campus student recruitment events \n• Travel to recruitment events as required \n• Collaborate with faculty, staff, and program coordinators to organize presentations, information \nsessions and recruitment related activities \n• Continually qualify the applicant pool \n• Monitor receipt of applications and support the prospective students throughout the admission process \nby maintaining contact, answering their queries, helping them complete required documents, and \nensuring file completion \n \n  \n \n• Review files for acceptance and present admission recommendations to the Admissions Coordinator \nand /or Director of External Relations \n• Effectively utilize the CRM (Customer Relationship Management) and Populi SIS (Student Information \nSystem) software to execute job responsibilities \n• Recruit and train BUC Brand Ambassadors \n \nQUALIFICATIONS \n1. Required \n• Satisfactory criminal record check \n• Fully supportive of the integration of faith and learning in a Christian university-level setting and a \ncommitment to the Mission of Booth University College  \n• Ability to integrate your Christian faith within the workplace \n• For admissions counsellor recruiting Salvationist students, must be a Salvationist in good standing \n• Post-secondary degree \n• Several years’ progressive work experience; experience in sales, marketing and/or student \nrecruitment preferred \n• Excellent communication and relation-building skills; with effective public speaking skills \n• Demonstrated ability to stay positively focused on attaining targets  \n• Capability to effectively utilize a complex database system  \n• Ability to work on registration deadlines, while meeting college-wide enrollment goals \n• Proficiency in utilizing customer service strategies to recruit students to Booth UC’s programs  \n• Willing to work flexible hours including some evenings  \n• Highly organized with excellent follow-up and creative problem-solving skills \n• A valid driver’s license and willing to travel  \n• Must be legally entitled to work in Canada \n \n2. Assets (Preferred Qualifications) \n• Experience with a Student Information System (SIS) or Customer Relations Management (CRM) \nsoftware  \n• A working understanding of The Salvation Army \n• Previous sales experience  \n \nThis is a full -time position with 37.5 hours per week.  In addition to the compensation package, we also \nprovide a benefits package that includes health, dental, and other group benefits, vacation and sick leave, \nRRSP, and a tuition bursary program.  Booth University College offers a stimulating university college \nenvironment, a mission-driven purpose, and the opportunity to contribute to change as Booth UC moves \nforward on its commitment to its mission:  Education for a Better World. \n \n  \n \n \nApplication Instructions \nApplications may be submitted in confidence to Human Resources; Booth University College by email: \nBoothUC.Careers@boothuc.ca.   \n \nApplications must include a cover letter. In the letter describe why you want to work at Booth University \nCollege. Also, please detail what key strengths you bring to this position.   \n \nAccommodations are available upon request throughout the recruitment process. \n \n \nAbout Booth University College \nBooth University College is a Christian university college located in Winnipeg, Manitoba (Canada) and rooted \nin The Salvation Army’s history of meeting the needs of the most vulnerable in society. As an open-enrollment \ninstitution, we welcome students from many different backgrounds, cultures , and countries. Deeply \ncommitted to social justice, we blend Christian faith and rigorous scholarship with a passion for service. Our \napproach to learning is anchored in academic excellence and based on a vision of social justice, hope , and \nmercy for all.  We are committed to our mission:  Education for a Better World .  This is described in our \nmission statement: \n \nWilliam and Catherine Booth University College, a Christian university college rooted in The Salvation Army’s \nWesleyan theological tradition, brings together Christian faith, rigorous scholarship, and a passion for service. \nThe University College educates students to understand the complexities of our world, to develop the \nknowledge and skills necessary to be active contributors to society, and to know how the Christian faith \ncompels them to bring hope, social justice, and mercy into our world. "
    },
    "https://boothuc.ca/assets/content/BUC%20Job%20Posting_VP%20Academic%20and%20Dean_Aug%202026.pdf": {
      "title": "Continuing Studies and any other academic deans, Director of Library Services, Registrar, Faculty (may be",
      "description": " \n \nVice President Academic and Dean \n \nThe Vice President Academic and Dean is the Chief Academic Officer of the institution and is a senior \nmember of the leadership team.  The Vice President Academic and Dean works together with the President \nand the University College Cabinet to implement the strategic goals of Booth University College.  The Vice \nPresident Academic and Dean leads, directs, and manages the academic programs of the institution and \nmust be able to work strategically, tactically, and operationally.  \n \nSUPERVISORY FUNCTIONS:  Supervises all academic departments and directly supervises Dean, School for \nContinuing Studies and any other academic deans, Director of Library Services, Registrar, Faculty (may be \ndelegated to a dean), Administrative Coordinator, and Coordinator, Field Education and Work Placement \nPrograms  \n \nESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS OF THE JOB/ROLES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: \n• Lead the development, implementation, and evaluation of Booth's academic strategic priorities, \nensuring alignment with Booth’s Strategic Plan \n• Provide leadership for institutional accreditation, quality assurance, academic assessment, and \ncontinuous improvement initiatives \n• Serve as a liaison between academic staff and administrative leadership \n• Oversee assessment of student learning outcomes, program effectiveness, and institutional \nacademic performance \n• Work closely with School Directors, Faculty, Student Services, and Students on issues of academic \nintegrity, academic probation, and student accessibility \n• Promote scholarship, research, and faculty professional development consistent with Booth's \nmission \n• Foster excellence in teaching through faculty mentoring, professional development, performance \nmanagement, and succession planning \n• Provide leadership for new programs and curriculum development, and for innovation in teaching \nand learning technologies  \n• Participate in institutional planning including enrollment, budgeting, risk management, and long-\nterm organizational sustainability \n• Champion academic excellence, student success, retention, and graduation outcomes. \n• Foster a collaborative, respectful, and mission-centered academic culture characterized by \nintegrity, collegiality, and servant leadership \n \n• Provide leadership and direction for academic affairs by setting goals and monitoring and ensuring \nimplementation \n• Strengthen the distinctive missional identity of Booth University College by ensuring programs, \ncourse curriculum, and other academic events align to the purpose and mission of Booth UC \n• Provide oversight on all academic appointments, tenure, and promotions, and ensure that \nprocesses comply with the Faculty Manual and other University College policies \n• Provide oversight, vision, and strategic direction in academic budgeting and staff planning. \n• In collaboration with the Dean of SCS, oversee the academic relationship with The Salvation Army \nCollege for Officer Training \n• Schedule and oversee academic program reviews and work with Deans and School Directors to \nstudy and develop reviews, improvement processes, and/or changes to academic policies. \n• Work with the President to strengthen relationships with The Salvation Army, accreditation bodies, \nexternal organizations, and other academic institutions \n• Represent the President in select forums as required  \n• Serve as Vice Chair of Senate and the Senate Planning & Priorities Committee. \n• Perform other duties as assigned by the President \n• Develop and maintain strong, positive relationships within The Salvation Army, both in the Canada \nand Bermuda Territory and internationally \n \n \nQUALIFICATIONS \n1. Required \n• PhD or other terminal degree \n• 7 years of senior or executive leadership experience \n• A scholar with a proven record of teaching and research  \n• Experienced manager with organizational abilities, financial acumen, proficient in change \nmanagement and conflict resolution \n• Fully supportive of the integration of faith and learning in a Christian university-level setting and a \ncommitment to the Mission of Booth University College  \n• Strong understanding of higher education in Canada (both public and private) with knowledge of \nthe Manitoba higher education landscape \n• Must have exceptional communication skills  \n• Must have the ability for strategic thinking  \n• Experience in governance and policy development  \n• Working knowledge of the church in Canada, including The Salvation Army.  \n• Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain networks and partnerships to facilitate goal \naccomplishments \n \n• Satisfactory criminal record check \n• Must be legally entitled to work in Canada \n \n2. Assets (Preferred Qualifications) \n• Working knowledge of various technologies (including AI), especially as they relate to higher \neducation  \n• A Salvationist in good standing \n \nAnticipated starting date for the position is November 1, 2026. \n \nApplication materials should include:  cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of faith and how your faith \ncommitment would contribute to fulfilling the mission of Booth UC, names and contact information for \nthree references.  \n \nApplications and requests for further information may be submitted in confidence to the Human Resources \nGeneralist; Booth University College by email: BoothUC.Careers@boothuc.ca \nApplications will be reviewed as received until the position is filled. \n \nAccommodations are available upon request throughout the recruitment process. \n \nAbout Booth University College \nBooth UC is a Christian university college located in Winnipeg, Manitoba (Canada) and rooted in The \nSalvation Army’s history of meeting the needs of the most vulnerable in society. As an open-enrollment \ninstitution, we welcome students from many different backgrounds, cultures, and countries. Deeply \ncommitted to social justice, we blend Christian faith and rigorous scholarship with a passion for service. Our \napproach to learning is anchored in academic excellence and based on a vision of social justice, hope, and \nmercy for all.  We are committed to our mission: Education for a Better World.  This is described in our \nmission statement: \n \nWilliam and Catherine Booth University College, a Christian university college rooted in The Salvation Army’s \nWesleyan theological tradition, brings together Christian faith, rigorous scholarship, and a passion for \nservice. The University College educates students to understand the complexities of our world, to develop \nthe knowledge and skills necessary to be active contributors to society, and to know how the Christian faith \ncompels them to bring hope, social justice, and mercy into our world. "
    },
    "https://boothuc.ca/assets/content/BUC%20Job%20Posting%20_Accessibility%20Manager%20(Part-time%2C%20Term).pdf": {
      "title": "As part of the Student Services department this position will give specific attention and focus to the needs of",
      "description": " \n \nAccessibility Manager (Part-time, term) \n \nAs part of the Student Services department this position will give specific attention and focus to the needs of \nstudents with temporary or permanent disability. This position will require strengthening relationships \nbetween the Academic office and Student Services as well as providing administrative support . \n \nSUPERVISORY FUNCTIONS: \nThis role has no supervisory functions. \n \nESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS OF THE JOB/ROLES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: \n \nA. Coordinate the Booth UC Accessibility Services Program \n• Meet with students requesting academic accommodation through Accessibility Services and ensure \ntheir full understanding of the process for program registration and accommodation support \nprovided.  \n• In accordance with policy, assess student needs based on documentation provided and approve \nrelevant accommodations to support their needs.  \n• Communicate with instructors concerning approved accommodations, providing guidance and \nadvocacy on appropriate measures for implementing accommodations when questions arise.  \n• Follow-up on specified tasks relevant to Accessibility Services, such as Volunteer Note Taker support \nor alternate format requests for textbooks.  \n• Coordinate transcription and ASL services for deaf and hard of hearing students.  \n• Stay informed of accessibility legislation and professional practice in accessibility services to ensure \nappropriate compliance with accessibility expectations. Maintain high level of service orientation \nand develop supports and processes that exceed general practice and create a learning environment \nin which students with disabilities can thrive. \n• Lead meetings of the Accessibility Working Group on campus.  \n• Report to Dean of Students on any accessibility concerns as they arise.  \n \nB. Coordinate Data and Feedback processes for the Student Services Department \n• In coordination with the Dean of Students develop and maintain policy and procedures considering \nAccreditation standards for Post Secondary Education.  \n• Develop and maintain data collection specific to student feedback, engagement and BoothUC policy \nand procedures \n• Provide general administrative support. \n• Invigilate exams as required. \n \nQUALIFICATIONS \n1. Required \n• Undergraduate (Bachelor) degree in education or relevant social science field as well as a working \nknowledge of the Canadian Standards within Human Rights legislation and Accessibility legislation. \n• Fully supportive of the integration of faith and learning in a Christian university-level setting and a \ncommitment to the Mission of Booth University College  \n \n• Ability to integrate your Christian faith within the workplace  \n• 3 years of related work experience (an acceptable combination of education and experience will be \nconsidered)  \n• Superior communication skills, both oral and written, and including demonstrated active listening \nskills  \n• Able to work in positive team-oriented manner with a variety of people  \n• Experience working with post-secondary students and/or adult learners  \n• Able to exercise discretion and judgement in sensitive and confidential matters  \n• Must be legally entitled to work in Canada \n \n2. Assets (Preferred Qualifications) \n• Knowledge of accessibility legislation and practice for accommodating students with disabilities  \n• Post-baccalaureate certification or degree in area related to disability studies, English as an \nadditional language, or other education specialization.  \n• Proficient in Microsoft Office (Word, Excel, Outlook and PowerPoint), \n• Strong organizational skills \n• Ability to document and organize information from multiple sources \n \nThis is a part-time, term position with up to 20 hours per week.  Booth University College offers a \nstimulating university college environment, a mission-driven purpose, and the opportunity to contribute to \nchange as Booth UC moves forward on its commitment to its mission:  Education for a Better World. \n \nApplication Instructions \nApplications may be submitted in confidence to Human Resources; Booth University College by email: \nBoothUC.Careers@boothuc.ca.   \nApplications must include a cover letter. In the letter describe why you want to work at Booth University \nCollege. Also, please detail what key strengths you bring to this position.   \n \nAccommodations are available upon request throughout the recruitment process. \n \n \nAbout Booth University College \nBooth University College is a Christian university college located in Winnipeg, Manitoba (Canada) and rooted \nin The Salvation Army’s history of meeting the needs of the most vulnerable in society. As an open-enrollment \ninstitution, we welcome students from many different backgrounds, cultures , and countries. Deeply \ncommitted to social justice, we blend Christian faith and rigorous scholarship with a passion for service. Our \napproach to learning is anchored in academic excellence and based on a vision of social justice, hope , and \nmercy for all.  We are committed to our mission:  Education for a Better World .  This is described in our \nmission statement: \n \nWilliam and Catherine Booth University College, a Christian university college rooted in The Salvation Army’s \nWesleyan theological tradition, brings together Christian faith, rigorous scholarship, and a passion for service. \nThe University College educates students to understand the complexities of our world, to develop the \nknowledge and skills necessary to be active contributors to society, and to know how the Christian faith \ncompels them to bring hope, social justice, and mercy into our world. "
    },
    "https://boothuc.ca/assets/content/BUC%20Job%20Posting_ALC%20Social%20Work%20Tutor%202026-2027-%20Updated%20Aug%202026.pdf": {
      "title": "Academic Learning Centre – Social Work Tutor Position",
      "description": " \nAcademic Learning Centre – Social Work Tutor Position  \n(2026-2027) \n \nBooth University College’s Academic Learning Centre (ALC) is designed to assist students with the \nacquisition of skills and strategies to help students grow in their abilities contributing to their academic \nsuccess.  \n \nThe ALC Social Work Tutor will work 6- 8 hours per week during the Fall and Winter academic terms. \n \nThe ALC Social Work Tutor will provide general academic support as well as subject-specific expertise in the \narea of Social Work. \n \nESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS OF THE JOB/ROLES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: \nAll tutors are expected to: \n• Understand or be eager to learn the ALC’s best practices and procedures \n• Help students with time management, developing study plans, creating assignment outlines, and \nunderstanding assignment requirements as well as engage with students through individualized \nfeedback on assignment drafts to help them ensure they are meeting task requirements, improve \ntheir clarity and grammar, and strengthen their central arguments in their writing \n• Assist students with the framework of Booth UC’s Social Work program, including IPT (Intern \nPlacement Tracking), practicum, learning objectives, etc. \n• Be able to help students use the correct style guide (APA, MLA, and Chicago) and navigate rules for \nformatting, citations, and reference pages \n• Be able to direct students to library resources, including navigating the library database \n• Attend and participate in tutor training sessions and staff meetings \n• Assist with invigilation of exams when requested  \n• Maintain all necessary records documenting student sessions and tutor hours in a timely manner \n \nQUALIFICATIONS \n1. Required \n• Bachelor of Social Work degree or equivalent \n• Registered Social Worker (preferred) or equivalent \n• Ability to take direction, initiative, collaborate, and work independently as needed under limited \nsupervision. \n• A strong grasp of grammar and academic writing, composition, and citation conventions  \n• Communicate clearly and concisely, verbally, and in writing \n• Understand and value academic integrity in post-secondary education \n• Cross-cultural awareness and ability to relate effectively with students from various cultures, \nacademic backgrounds, and aptitudes \n• Excellent time management strategies \n• Good understanding of concepts and material related to Booth UC programs of study \n• Good understanding of how to conduct academic research, including use of library resources and \nsystems \n• Commitment to helping others improve their own abilities \n• Attention to detail and ability to read carefully; ability to track and submit reports on work done \n \n2. Assets (Preferred Qualifications) \n• Previous tutoring or teaching experience  \n• A clear understanding of the responsibilities, goals, and pedagogy of the Academic Learning Centre \n• Ability to quickly develop positive rapport with others and be an effective team contributor \n• Ability to take initiative \n \nHow to Apply:  \nIf you are interested in applying for this position, please submit the following items to \nBoothUC.Careers@BoothUC.ca : \n1. A cover letter that includes the following:  \na. A clear note indicating an application as ALC Social Work Tutor \nb. A statement of the reasons you are applying for this position  \nc. Highlight any specific skills or experience that make you an asset to the ALC (Academic \nLearning Center) \n2. Resume of work and volunteer experience  \nQuestions regarding this position may be directed to the Dean of Students, Barb Stanley at \nBarb.Stanley@boothuc.ca.  \nAccommodations are available upon request throughout the recruitment process. \n \nAbout Booth University College \nBooth UC is a Christian university college located in Winnipeg, Manitoba (Canada) and rooted in The Salvation \nArmy’s history of meeting the needs of the most vulnerable in society. As an open-enrollment institution, we \nwelcome students from many different backgrounds, cultures , and countries. Deeply committed to social \njustice, we blend Christian faith and rigorous scholarship with a passion for service. Our approach to learning \nis anchored in academic excellence and based on a vision of social justice, hope , and mercy for all.  We are \ncommitted to our mission:  Education for a Better World.  This is described in our mission statement: \n \nWilliam and Catherine Booth University College, a Christian university college rooted in The Salvation Army’s \nWesleyan theological tradition, brings together Christian faith, rigorous scholarship, and a passion for service. \nThe University College educates students to understand the complexities of our world, to develop the \nknowledge and skills necessary to be active contributors to society, and to know how the Christian faith \ncompels them to bring hope, social justice, and mercy into our world. \n "
    },
    "https://boothuc.ca/assets/content/BUC%20Job%20Posting_Sessional%20Instructor%20(for%20MBA)_Feb%2027%202026.pdf": {
      "title": "Sessional Instructor",
      "description": " \n \nBooth University College \nSessional Instructor  \n \nThe MBA Program of Booth University College invites applications for multiple Sessional Instructor \npositions in Business Administration (with a variety of specializations in Marketing, Accounting, Data \nAnalyses & Research Methods, CSR, Leadership). The successful candidates will design and teach courses \nacross the Master of Business Administration program. Preference will be given to applicants with \ndemonstrated experience in graduate-level Business programs. \n \nCore Responsibilities \nSessional faculty members are expected to prepare a syllabus, including course description, learning \noutcomes, course outline and assessment strategies using the Booth University College Syllabus \nTemplate.  Sample syllabi will be provided to the successful candidates. Upon approval of the syllabus by \nthe School Director and the Academic Office, detailed lesson plans are prepared in advance of course \noffering and posted to Populi (LMS), together with all relevant materials, including handouts, assigned \nreading, assignments and assessment rubrics. \n \nSpecifically, a Sessional Instructor is responsible to: \n• Engage with students to provide academic support, clarify course material, and foster a \nresponsive learning environment \n• Deliver instruction in in-person, virtual and hybrid formats, combining instruction and \ntechnology through teaching methods, practices and tools and strong teaching strategies \n• Collaborate effectively with program administration through clear communication, \ndepartmental meetings, and submission of report and documentation \n• Develop and grade course exams and assignments to ensure alignment with learning objectives, \nacademic standards and institutional polices while utilizing the SIS system \n \nQualifications:  \nAll applicants must possess a PhD/DBA or hold equivalent teaching experience at the graduate level . \nCandidates with work in progress in doctoral studies will be considered if there is evidence of extensive \nteaching experience. (Candidates with a combination of CPA and MBA will be considered for accounting \ncourses with a minimum of five years of executive work experience.) Satisfactory Criminal Record Check \ncertificate must be provided. The successful candidate must have an active Christian faith and a \ncommitment to the integration of faith and learning in a Christian university-level setting. \n \n \n\nTo Apply \n \nApplication materials should include: \n• curriculum vitae,  \n• statement of teaching philosophy,  \n• statement on how your faith commitment would contribute to both teaching and scholarship \nat Booth UC,  \n• names and contact information for three references and  \n• evidence of effective teaching ( provide samples of course syllabi, teaching outlines and other \nmaterials, if available).   \n \nComplete application materials stated above must be submitted in confidence directly to the Human \nResources Generalist, at: (email: BoothUC.Careers@BoothUC.ca). \n \nAll qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however in accordance with Canadian Immigration \nrequirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority.  Applications will be reviewed as \nreceived, until the position is filled. \n \nAccommodations are available upon request throughout the recruitment process. \n \nAbout Booth University College \nBooth University College is a Christian institution centrally located in Winnipeg. The University College is \nrooted in The Salvation Army’s history of meeting the needs of the most vulnerable in society and are \ncommitted to “Education for a Better World” as described in our mission statement: \n \nWilliam and Catherine Booth University College, a Christian university college rooted in The Salvation \nArmy’s Wesleyan theological tradition, brings together Christian faith, rigorous scholarship and a passion \nfor service. The University College educates s tudents to understand the complexities of our world, to \ndevelop the knowledge and skills necessary to be active contributors to society, and to know how Christian \nfaith compels them to bring hope, social justice and mercy into our world. \n \n  "
    }
  },
